import type { V205BookData } from '../types';

export const DATA: V205BookData = {
  book: "Jude",
  display: "Jude",
  chapters: {
    "1": {"chapter":1,"intro":[{"kind":"para","text":"I should start by describing what an “apostasy” is. I won’t be using that term very much, but it is the main theme of this book. An “apostasy” means “To reject or abandon the belief of a specific culture or religion.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"This book was written by a man named Judas (Jude being the English name for him), the brother of James (which he will mention in a moment). Scripture helps us identify which Judas this can be out of the four men who go by the name of James:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Is not this the carpenter's son? is not his mother called Mary? and his brethren, James, and Joses, and Simon, and Judas?","ref":"Matthew 13:55"},{"kind":"para","text":"Obviously, this insinuates that this is the Lord’s half-brother Judas, who was also James’ brother (who wrote the book of James). There were only two other Judas’ that we know of (one is obvious), one is only known by this:"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Judas saith unto him, not Iscariot, Lord, how is it that thou wilt manifest thyself unto us, and not unto the world?","ref":"John 14:22"},{"kind":"para","text":"Interestingly enough, neither James nor Judas identify themselves as being the Lord’s brother. James simply says that he is “a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ…” No doubt, they didn’t talk about their relationship with the Lord in that way because they now believed in who He said He was. They didn’t believe Him before His death. They were more than likely not even a part of following Him during all this time of ministry."},{"kind":"para","text":"It was after His resurrection and ascension that they believed, and it is in that relationship they hold much higher in value than their sinful rejection of Him before His ascension. Jesus’ brother (as well as Mary and others) had to accept the Lord just as everyone else."},{"kind":"para","text":"That’s not what I find the most interesting about this short letter, though. What I find interesting about this letter is that Jude labels some things that are not found anywhere else in scripture. It has been said, “The best commentary for the Bible is the Bible.” But what do you do when you can’t back the Bible up with itself? Can we take the words penned here as literal then? Is this a trustworthy text?"},{"kind":"para","text":"I think the best part of this letter is where it is located, which is just before the prophecies of Revelation. This book is a great introduction to the intricacies and spiraling text found in the book that follows it."}],"previously":[],"outline":[],"sections":[{"start":1,"end":2,"title":"Assurance For Believers","entries":[{"start":1,"end":1,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"In the Greek, the term for “sanctified” is mixed up with the term for “loved.” We could easily interchange sanctify for love, and it would honestly be more appropriately used here. We should take it as a treasure to know that we aren’t just sanctified by the Lord, but we are loved by the Lord who is our Father. Obviously, the start of this Epistle is written to a group of Christians who need to know they are loved. 2 Mercy unto you, and peace, and love, be multiplied."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is no ill-will here. Jude desires that mercy, peace, and love would be multiplied to the readers of this letter. We sometimes can group these words together and think they all fall under the same category of God’s character, and not get the full effect of the words being used. These three words are separate and they should be treated as such."},{"kind":"para","text":"The love of God is displayed by His mercy, and the peace of God only comes because we are resting in His love. God’s peace will never be given to those that don’t know His love. And how can we understand the depths of God’s love without first accepting His mercy, which was proven to us through the salvation of Jesus Christ? Grace, love, and mercy are all part of God’s salvation, but they cannot exist one without the other."}]}]},{"start":3,"end":3,"title":"Change Subject to Apostasy","entries":[{"start":3,"end":3,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"It seems that Jude was originally planning to write to his Christian brothers to help deepen their understanding of salvation. He starts off by saying, “My intentions were to give as much diligence (or attention) to write about the simplicity of the gospel message, which is the salvation of Jesus Christ.” He must have seen that there was a strong need to strengthen their faith by going back to their first love."},{"kind":"para","text":"Or maybe he saw that there were many who were very confused, and those who knew the simplicity of the gospel (because the gospel really is that simple) would be able to stand up against those who were trying to sway others into a different thought about what scripture really and truly meant. He desired that this group would fix their eyes upon Jesus and get their thoughts out of unnatural and undesirable things. Why? Well, he answers that in the next verse…"}]}]},{"start":4,"end":4,"title":"Occurrences of Apostasy","entries":[{"start":4,"end":4,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Let it be known that Satan doesn’t come to steal, kill, and destroy with blowing a horn and making it obvious. Because Satan’s a creep, he will creep into the conversation. He’s subtle. He doesn’t like to attract attention until he’s able to get you completely distracted first. Oh, he enjoys the attention, but he enjoys the division more. Whatever he can do to take the eyes off of Christ would suit him well."},{"kind":"para","text":"The funny part about Satan, though, is that he does the same thing over and over again. He can be seen from a mile away at this point to a Christian who has contended with Satan in the past. Where does he creep in, though? He doesn’t creep into the homeless on the street corner. He doesn’t creep into satanic religious practices. He doesn’t creep into politics or cults. That only leaves one place to creep… Our churches."},{"kind":"para","text":"He is all too obvious everywhere else, but he makes sure to stay down low when creeping into the congregation or into the office of the pastor. That’s when Satan’s attack hurts the most because you are expecting it the least there, and Jude is drawing attention to it!"}]}]},{"start":5,"end":5,"title":"Israel’s Unbelief","entries":[{"start":5,"end":5,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"This is a great recollection. God performs miracle after miracle for His children, and they consistently turn their backs on Him. Even in Egypt, we see this happen time and time again. If you want to go on a rollercoaster ride of revival and falling, read the books of Kings. It displays God’s children (not people who don’t know God, but people who do and who have seen God work time and time again) rise high up for following the Lord, and then drop far down for not following Him. Jude is relating that situation to today."},{"kind":"para","text":"We (who know Christ) can just as easily fall away and be left out of God’s blessings because we have believed some new truth (which is actually a lie, but Satan likes to disguise his lies in half-truths). Strangely enough, the Bible gives us warning after warning about this entering into the church, and we still fall for it because we expect the people of God to act like the people of God. They should, yes, but it’s proven over and over that they don’t."},{"kind":"para","text":"The Apostles had special power to give them continuance in the church that none of us seem to have today. Yet, I do believe it’s entirely possible for us to achieve continuation as far as I’m concerned. We can be far better suited for the continuation of ministry and the gospel if we would stop listening to other men and start studying the Word of God. Take the downfalls of the Bible’s titans of the past and use them as lessons, not as an opportunity to twist scripture!"}]}]},{"start":6,"end":6,"title":"Angels Kept In Chains","entries":[{"start":6,"end":6,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Now we’ve entered into a thought in this letter that is not mentioned anywhere else in scripture. Of course, we know that there will be judgment for angels. We are also alluded to the acts and services of fallen angels through the ministry of Christ (i.e., possessions and the sorts). We understand that angels have free will and are created by God for services to Him specifically. Jesus sent demons into pigs to drown, but there’s no more mention about what happens to the demons after the fact. Do they leave to go torture someone else? Are they dead and vanished forever, never to be seen again? There’s no clear-cut answer, but I think this gives an insinuation to what happens to them after they leave this Earth."},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems they are “chained” and kept in darkness, which is another way to say they were cast to Hell. We understand this to be Hell because Peter mentioned it as well!"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"For if God spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment;","ref":"2 Peter 2:4"},{"kind":"para","text":"People see Hell as a lake of fire, and it certainly is, because we are told that it is…"},{"kind":"scripture","text":"Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, ye cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:","ref":"Matthew 25:41"},{"kind":"para","text":"It seems it will also be dark. It will have the heat and radiance of fire, but you will not see anything. More torturous than fire itself is being in pain, blind, with nowhere to go and nothing to hold onto. Hell is not a dungeonous place like some movies portray it. It’s a dark place with no ends to it, void of light and freedom. It will be a place where we would hear screaming from pain, yet there would be no way to reach those screaming, and no way for us to communicate with them because of our own pain."},{"kind":"para","text":"The angels will be there until the day of judgment comes (which will be when Christ is on Earth reigning over it for 1,000 years) and then they will be released to torment and drag others to Hell with them."}]}]},{"start":7,"end":7,"title":"Sodom and Gomorrah","entries":[{"start":7,"end":7,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jude uses another past biblical event to illustrate the neglect of scriptural leadership in the church. He warns them of Sodom and Gomorrah and the example they left. It would seem that this church was engaging in sexually immoral acts in the name of “religious practice,” believing it was a biblical trait because some man told them it was."},{"kind":"para","text":"Either way, there are standards that Christians are to uphold, whether the leaders of the church are willing to or not. Jude desires that there would be a “few good men” who would hold up the faith with a strong desire to glorify Christ as Lord and Savior, over religious sacrifice or vain and sinful practices."}]}]},{"start":8,"end":10,"title":"Modern Apostate","entries":[{"start":8,"end":8,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Some choice words are used to describe these false teachers coming in among the flock of God. He first calls them “filthy dreamers.” This can have a whole arch of indication. It could be a reference to some sort of sexual immorality that was being taught, judging by the verse directly before this which speaks about that, or (and more likely the case) it is just referencing them as living in an “unreal” reality. It’s like modern-day preachers and the prosperity gospel. They think that spiritual gifts are given by tangible rewards. Sometimes God can gift us tangibly, but most times God gifts us spiritually or provisionally."},{"kind":"para","text":"He then says they “defile the flesh.” Once again, I believe this goes back to the sexual immorality in the verse before. That would make the most sense; they have an addiction or a coping mechanism with lust or adultery and have tried to justify that sin through twisting God’s Word."},{"kind":"para","text":"The third thing we are told that identifies these false teachers is their despise for dominion. This really just means they reject authority very blatantly. They would probably do it under the pretense that they are serving the Lord and don’t need the approval of authority (even knowing that it goes directly against authority)."},{"kind":"para","text":"Then we can point out false teachers by the last identity which is “speaking evil of dignities.” That’s (once again) a way of saying they have nothing but terrible things to say about authority, simply because they are authority. “We’ve had a bad president, so it’s time to get rid of the office of the president because all presidents will be bad.” -type of mentality."},{"kind":"para","text":"Usually, these types are ones that complain about the authority over them, but only to their TVs. They aren’t doing anything to initiate real change in their communities, but complain because no one is doing anything either. We should be the forefront (as Christians) making real change, even if it’s small change, so that it can lead to something much bigger. This is, may I remind you, IN THE CHURCH. This isn’t some sort of political office, or even in secular colleges or workplaces. This is in the holy temples of God."},{"kind":"para","text":"Out of a poll of 700 preachers, the following results were given: 48% denied the complete inspiration of the Bible; 24% rejected the atonement; 12% rejected the resurrection of the body; 27% did not believe that Christ will return to judge the quick and the dead. These are sad numbers, but they must not surprise us. It was already cultivating during the time of the writing of this letter from Jude, and obviously widely noticed from John and other Apostles as well. 9 Yet Michael the archangel, when contending with the devil he disputed about the body of Moses, durst not bring against him a railing accusation, but said, The Lord rebuke thee."},{"kind":"para","text":"One of those verses that are not mentioned anywhere else in the Bible! There is so much that could be addressed in this verse, and I want to do my best to give it justice. We notice that Michael, the archangel, is disputing with Satan about the body of Moses, but he doesn’t overthrow the authority of Satan. Rather, he tells Satan that the argument is futile and that if he wants the answer to his question, he should take it up with God."},{"kind":"para","text":"The argument on where the body should be buried was for a few reasons. One, because God said that Moses wouldn’t enter the promised land, so if the body was buried there, it would make God a liar. Two, because if Satan got his way, he would be able to hinder Moses from appearing at the Mount of Transfiguration. Three, Satan believes he’s the master of the Earth and grounds, and so it was up to him to decide the burial spot of Moses. It was the Lord that ended up taking the body and burying it where He saw fit."},{"kind":"para","text":"See, Michael could have pinned other angels against Satan at this point, but Michael didn’t do that. Michael knew his position compared to Lucifer, but he also knew that the Lord would deal with the matters if we would just let Him. And God can deal with matters far better than we can in our own hands and power. 10 But these speak evil of those things which they know not: but what they know naturally, as brute beasts, in those things they corrupt themselves."},{"kind":"para","text":"Causing disputing, splitting hairs, and seeking to create a culture of bitterness and pride is what they know naturally. They make fun of or speak evil of what they don’t know. They don’t know the love of God, so they make fun of it. They don’t know the faith of God, so they hate it. They don’t know the mercy of God, so they despise it. They don’t know the Will of God, so they forsake it. These things they don’t know because the Lord has not let them know."},{"kind":"para","text":"You can learn a lot from Bible college, and there’s a lot you can do in seminary through the power and might of God, but you will not learn how to love like God, how to grow your faith in God, how to show mercy like God, or how to follow God’s Will in Bible college. Those are things that God reveals to you through a relationship with Him. You cannot learn them; you can only grow in them through Christ and following Him."}]}]},{"start":11,"end":16,"title":"Apostate Described","entries":[{"start":11,"end":11,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jude is, yet again, drawing from the Old Testament examples of bad practices. I don’t think I have to explain the lifestyle of Cain. We all know about the first murder. You may not know so much about Balaam or Core. Balaam was also an Old Testament figure. He was a prophet, but of the opposite effect. The best way to describe Balaam is as McGee describes him, “In the Book of Revelation, you have the doctrine of Balaam.”"},{"kind":"para","text":"The churches were suffering from some serious faith issues and false teachings, doing heinous and despicable things in the name of their god. Balaam was the master at getting people to do just that. Balaam desired to earn money for his prophecies, and that would ultimately be his reward for it. He squandered a gift that was given to him by the Lord and abused people who respected his position. The worst part is, people fell for it and loved it!"},{"kind":"para","text":"Core is Korah from the Old Testament. He led a rebellion against Moses in Numbers 16. Korah came to the conclusion that Moses was not the only one around who had access to God. So, because of that, he went against God’s given authority and tried to overthrow it. All of these men of the Old Testament proved to be nothing more than bad examples of what to avoid. They are also the representation of bad teachers and pastors today. There are many pastors who have one, two, or even all three of these traits in their pastorates, and Jude points out how damnable and dangerous it was for them, and how damnable and dangerous it can be for us. 12 These are spots in your feasts of charity, when they feast with you, feeding themselves without fear: clouds they are without water, carried about of winds; trees whose fruit withereth, without fruit, twice dead, plucked up by the roots;"},{"kind":"para","text":"I could talk about each trait through and through. Feasting with other believers but abusing the cause, like getting drunk with wine when we are to be celebrating with it. Clouds that look beautiful and think they will provide substance to the Earth, but hold no water, like a car with no engine. Trees with no fruit."},{"kind":"para","text":"The part that really catches my interest is when he says, “…twice dead, plucked up by the roots.” D.L. Moody said, “When a man is born once, he will have to die twice. When a man is born twice, he will have to die only once.” 13 Raging waves of the sea, foaming out their own shame; wandering stars, to whom is reserved the blackness of darkness for ever."},{"kind":"para","text":"The imagery here shouldn’t be missed. The false preacher and bad pastors are given this image of foaming like the sea, but the only thing that foams out of a false preacher is shame. Again, sea foam looks beautiful on the outside, but the only thing that washes up on shore is dead fish and trash. Same with wandering stars. They are here for a moment and then disappear into darkness and nothingness forever. I can’t find any better words to describe false preachers. 14 And Enoch also, the seventh from Adam, prophesied of these, saying, Behold, the Lord cometh with ten thousands of his saints, 15 To execute judgment upon all, and to convince all that are ungodly among them of all their ungodly deeds which they have ungodly committed, and of all their hard speeches which ungodly sinners have spoken against him."},{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s another verse that is not found proven in the Old Testament. Enoch wasn’t a common name in the Bible, so it’s pretty clear that the Enoch that Jude mentioned here is the same Enoch that walked with God and then was taken. Imagine this though! If this is the Enoch of Genesis, that means he was prophesying about the false teachers of the last days from the beginning. He was a compassionate preacher back then!"},{"kind":"para","text":"He speaks about the coming of Christ with His saints, which is then further clarified by John in Revelation."},{"kind":"scripture","text":"And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse; and he that sat upon him was called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he doth judge and make war. His eyes were as a flame of fire, and on his head were many crowns; and he had a name written, that no man knew, but he himself. And he was clothed with a vesture dipped in blood: and his name is called The Word of God. And the armies which were in heaven followed him upon white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean.","ref":"Revelation 19:11-14"},{"kind":"para","text":"This more than likely is the reason that God took him far before his time. He was just too passionate about the Word of God in a world that could care less about God. This is also why, I believe, God will take us in the last days. 16 These are murmurers, complainers, walking after their own lusts; and their mouth speaketh great swelling words, having men's persons in admiration because of advantage."},{"kind":"para","text":"Jude provides five more identities to the false preachers:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Murmurers Complainers Lusters Gloaters Prideful (They enjoy applause from others)."},{"kind":"para","text":"We should have a count by the end of this of all of the obvious signs of an apostate, and have them on record so we can know who is true and who is just playing the part. You think they would be easy to spot, but they are often very hard because we are easily deceivable."}]}]},{"start":17,"end":19,"title":"Warning to Believers","entries":[{"start":17,"end":18,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jesus warned of it. The Apostles warned of it. Jude, here, is warning of it. The book of Revelation displays the effect it causes when things are not changed. There’s no reason for us not to fall for it… right?"}]},{"start":19,"end":19,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Jude says, “Remember what we were warned about? Remember what Christ told you to look out for? You know those identities I gave to them? Hey, they are those people right in front of you. They are right here! Don’t miss them, because apparently it’s easy to miss them.” It’s just as hard for us to see them too, because we desire to trust the people who are supposed to lead us."},{"kind":"para","text":"It’s not a far cry to say that people of God should trust the leaders of God with leading them for God. Yet, here we are. There they were. And it was evident that there were many in the church falling for the tricks and traps of false preachers."}]}]},{"start":20,"end":25,"title":"What the Believers Should Do","entries":[{"start":20,"end":21,"blocks":[{"kind":"para","text":"Here’s what we should be doing rather than falling for and following after the wrong teachings and the bad leadership:"},{"kind":"para","text":"Building up your spiritual house (which is by faith). Praying in the Holy Ghost (including for those that are against you, and desire to hurt you). Remembering that God loves you and staying in that love desiring to please Him because you love Him in return. Seeking mercy (the only mercy that is worth seeking is from the Lord, because we know that His mercy is given without requirement), which is ultimately what provides us with eternal life. And then… this next one hits hard… 22 And of some have compassion, making a difference:"},{"kind":"para","text":"The false preacher will not have compassion. They will have a lot of terribly bad things to do and say, but compassion is not one of them. We, though (who are in Christ), can have compassion. And you know what our compassion does? It makes a difference! It changes the game! It causes relief and humility! It makes suffering more bearable and tolerable. Compassion can cause the hardest of hearts to soften. And no one can give compassion like a true child of God. 23 And others save with fear, pulling them out of the fire; hating even the garment spotted by the flesh."},{"kind":"para","text":"There is a time when we have to dive into the ankles of our loved ones for their eternity’s sake. We don’t want them to die in their sins, and that is exactly what they will do. We can do nothing but hold onto their legs and plead with them, fighting with them to get out and away from the fire… 24 Now unto him that is able to keep you from falling, and to present you faultless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy, 25 To the only wise God our Saviour, be glory and majesty, dominion and power, both now and ever. Amen."},{"kind":"para","text":"Why do that? Why build up these traits? Because it glorifies God! When God is glorified, it brings us great joy. When God is glorified, we are blessed. When God is glorified, we are comforted. When God is glorified, we are safe. When God is glorified, we don’t suffer; we encourage."}]}]}],"stories":[{"start":1,"end":1},{"start":3,"end":3},{"start":4,"end":4},{"start":5,"end":5},{"start":6,"end":6},{"start":7,"end":7},{"start":8,"end":8},{"start":11,"end":11},{"start":17,"end":18},{"start":19,"end":19},{"start":20,"end":21}],"verses":{"1":"Jude, the servant of Jesus Christ, and brother of James, to them that are sanctified by God the Father, and preserved in Jesus Christ, and called:","3":"Beloved, when I gave all diligence to write unto you of the common salvation, it was needful for me to write unto you, and exhort you that ye should earnestly contend for the faith which was once delivered unto the saints.","4":"For there are certain men crept in unawares, who were before of old ordained to this condemnation, ungodly men, turning the grace of our God into lasciviousness, and denying the only Lord God, and our Lord Jesus Christ.","5":"I will therefore put you in remembrance, though ye once knew this, how that the Lord, having saved the people out of the land of Egypt, afterward destroyed them that believed not.","6":"And the angels which kept not their first estate, but left their own habitation, he hath reserved in everlasting chains under darkness unto the judgment of the great day.","7":"Even as Sodom and Gomorrha, and the cities about them in like manner, giving themselves over to fornication, and going after strange flesh, are set forth for an example, suffering the vengeance of eternal fire.","8":"Likewise also these filthy dreamers defile the flesh, despise dominion, and speak evil of dignities.","11":"Woe unto them! for they have gone in the way of Cain, and ran greedily after the error of Balaam for reward, and perished in the gainsaying of Core.","17":"But, beloved, remember ye the words which were spoken before of the apostles of our Lord Jesus Christ;","18":"How that they told you there should be mockers in the last time, who should walk after their own ungodly lusts.","19":"These be they who separate themselves, sensual, having not the Spirit.","20":"But ye, beloved, building up yourselves on your most holy faith, praying in the Holy Ghost,","21":"Keep yourselves in the love of God, looking for the mercy of our Lord Jesus Christ unto eternal life."}},
  },
};
